Fire Emblem fates birthright is the  perfect game for newbies to the series  gameplay isn't easy but it is forgiving  with multiple customization options for  difficulty it also presents a story that  isn't complex so much as it is heartfelt  and although the writing can get  melodramatic at times it does elicit  genuine bursts of emotions frustration  anxiety sadness it's all there  built neatly onto the framework of a  strategy role-playing games stuffed with  tactical puzzles that yield satisfying  results in short it's everything you've  ever loved about Fire Emblem with a  handful of new mechanical twists and  works birth rate begins with your  customizable protagonists heading out  with your four siblings on a mission for  your father King Garon of noir but  surprise you're actually not their  sister but a royal daughter of the  hoshido Kingdom a neighbor at war with  noir after a brief skirmish you are  reunited with your hoshido siblings and  given a magic sword for being sent out  into the battlefield here you are given  the choice of three separate narrative  paths and if you pick birthright then  you've chosen to remain with your blood  family and fight the people who have  raised you talk about biting the hand  that feeds you this sounds like a really  cheesy premise but it creates a canvas  on which some truly affecting moments  occur birthright follows the time-tested  Fire Emblem format of turn-based battles  playing out on a grid you move your  characters like chess pieces weighing  their strengths and weaknesses against  your enemies when engaging an enemy the  bottom 3ds screen will display how their  weapons stack up to yours as well as the  potential damage you inflict on each  other and you can also highlight any  opponent to see their range of movement  this is exceedingly helpful when  planning out moves especially when  you're looking to protect weaker units  birth rate also emphasizes that same  kind of unit placement introduced in  awakening we're placing units next to  each other will often allow them to team  up and get a few extra hits in on  enemies  selecting from your dozens of fighters  and coordinating your assault is a  delicate dance and forces you to agonize  over every move it's a special kind of  anxiety that makes you feel  accomplishment with each battle one like  untying a particularly convoluted knot  while birthrates maps sadly don't have  much variation the plethora of  activities you can do back at your home  base do the my castle feature allows you  to build a strongholds complete with  armories a mess hall lottery shop and  arena as well as your private quarters  where you can get more intimate with  your recruits and boy can things get  intimate as units fight together in  battle they can build up a bond that  makes them a better team on the field  and in the sheets if it gets strong  enough offspring resulting from these  unions will show up in optional  side-quests called paralogs this feature  made me feel closer to my team as all  units are given the opportunity to speak  with one another in the support menu  which tracks their budding relationships  often I would find characters roaming  the grounds together harvesting daikon  or running the mess hall then I would  come home to my husband and do  ridiculous things like rub his face and  blow on the 3ds mic to stir up bath  steam this gives you a deeper connection  to your people which adds to the lovely  theatricality of the story and makes it  that much harder to see them fall in  battle you can play a few ways in  birthright if this is your first Fire  Emblem Phoenix mode is a wonderful more  gentle on the nerves addition that  allows you to experience the story with  minimal agony Phoenix mode resurrects  your fallen units after every turn which  also allows them to survive nearly every  battle and keep all experience points  and items they earn this along with the  scouting feature makes the grind easier  scouting randomly generates optional  challenge missions on the world map  giving you the opportunity to spend  extra time levelling certain units or  preparing others for Parenthood scouting  allowed me to not only level up when I  was too underpowered to progress the  story and also spend time forging  relationships between certain units in  this way I could bring my army up to  snuff with minimal fuss and also have a  simplified way of breeding my army of  super strong children all three fates  games also let you drop the difficulty  mode at any time if things get a little  too much for you so if you're easing  into the fates experience or if you only  care about the story and the romance  stuff birthright is a great start but  compared to its sister games Fire Emblem  fates conquest and revelation birthright  gives you more access to resources and  allows you to recruit more units so many  people want to help you and you'll have  a variety of heavy-hitter and spell  casters at your disposal I like to my  embarrassment of riches because it  allowed me to tinker with my characters  classes and weapon sets as well as buy  them cute accessories or make them walk  around in bath towels it really gives  you the feeling of running a small army  as you tend to their emotional and  physical needs but  in and out of combat did I mention at  any point that your avatar can also turn  into a dragon I didn't because it  doesn't matter turning into a dragon and  combat gives you a bit of a physical  advantage in physical attacks but not  much and after your initial  transformation in the story it doesn't  acknowledge your weird habit ever again  it makes the whole schtick about you  being the chosen something or other feel  cheap and its glaring lack of importance  highlight some of the other cheesier  melodrama of birthrights  like subplots about treason and your  childhood best friend whom you forgot  but the melodrama works for birthright  especially in the context of the  building a ragtag army to take down a  despotic leaders story fire emblems  over-the-top portrayals of heroism love  and sacrifice add drama to fighting with  sprites on a grid and in birthright the  cheese is turned up to 11 it's pretty  great  if you loved its predecessor Fire Emblem  awakening you'll enjoy the heightened  tension and intimacy imbued in every  moment of Fire Emblem fates birthright  between the strategy that dating sim  elements that can help but swoon over at  melodrama and some truly gorgeous  visuals and music you can overlook  things like repetitive maps and  cringe-worthy dumb parts of the writing  the series that kicked off the strategy  RPG genre still has it and you'll enjoy  getting wrapped up in the conflict
 

